{"text": "11/25/98\n\nIn Vitro Short-Term Tests\n\nThe ICH standard battery for short-term tests on pharmaceuticals consists of (1) a test for\ngene mutation in bacteria, (2) an in vitro test with cytogenetic evaluation of chromosomal\ndamage with mammalian cells or an in vitro mouse lymphoma tk assay, and (3) an in vivo\ntest for chromosomal damage using rodent hematopoietic cells (ICH, 1997).\nFrom the genotoxicity assays, the Ames and the chromosome aberration assays show a\nbroad dynamic range and therefore they can be used as quantitative tests for cigarette\ndiscrimination. The Ames and the CA assays are well validated and are recommended by\nregulatory authorities. The in vitro micronucleus and the mouse lymphoma assays re-\nspond to cigarette smoke, but only with yes/no response.\n\nThe neutral red uptake cytotoxicity assay shows a dynamic range and therefore it can be\nused as quantitative test for olgarette discrimination.\n\nIt should be mentioned that all assays should be well validated and carried out according\nto standard operation procedures and monitored for good laboratory practice by a quailty\n\nassurance unit.\n\n8h6LZ6920z",
